    "indian-startups-037cd803c2": {
      "headline": "Climatetech startup CarbonStrong raises Rs 12.5 crore from IAN Angel Fund, Zerodha's Rainmatter, others",
      "source": "Indian Startup News",
      "url": "https://indianstartupnews.com/funding/climatetech-startup-carbonstrong-raises-rs-125-crore-from-ian-angel-fund-zerodhas-rainmatter-others-12432614",
      "published": "2026-08-25T10:46:08+00:00",
      "image": null,
      "fulltext": "CarbonStrong, a Bengaluru-based climatetech startup developing low-carbon cement substitutes from industrial waste, has raised Rs 12.5 crore in a funding round led by IAN Angel Fund, with participation from Rainmatter, Social Alpha, Spectrum Impact and Full Circle Ventures.\nThe startup said the funding will help it in setting up its first production facility, expanding its team, undertaking further product development and testing, and moving from customer trials to commercial-scale production. It aims to build a capacity of up to 100,000 tonnes a year over the next 2 years.\nFounded in 2022 by Harsh Jain (Co-founder & CEO) and Vikramaditya Singh (Co- founder & COO), CarbonStrong produces low carbon binders by upcycling industrial wastes to replace up to 50% cement in concrete. The startup said the binder is made by processing industrial wastes from coal, steel and other sectors into high-performance cementitious materials. This cuts cost and carbon footprint and maintains performance of concrete with no change to plants or process, it said.\nHarsh Jain, Co-founder and CEO, CarbonStrong, said, “India will build most of its future in the next 25-30 years. We want every ton of that concrete to be stronger, cost- efficient, and lower in carbon footprint. This investment helps us take our now-proven technology to industrial scale.”\nCarbonStrong further said it has conducted several customer trials and paid pilots. The startup is now looking to convert these early trials into long-term supply relationships with ready-mix concrete companies, builders and contractors. It is also targeting precast concrete and paver block manufacturers.\nIt is also working to expand the range of industrial wastes that can be turned into useful cement substitutes.\nCarbonStrong's future plans include materials made from steel slag, copper slag, mine-tailings and other industrial byproducts.",
      "text_source": "full"
    },
