    "beyond-your-beat-a1169c363b": {
      "headline": "Loitering munition may have hit Kirana Hills during Operation Sindoor: former CDS Anil Chauhan",
      "source": "The Hindu",
      "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/loitering-munition-may-have-hit-kirana-hills-during-operation-sindoor-former-cds-anil-chauhan/article71390353.ece",
      "published": "2026-08-25T20:14:40+00:00",
      "image": null,
      "fulltext": "Former Chief of Defence Staff (CDS) General Anil Chauhan has said a loitering munition deployed during Operation Sindoor may have struck the Kirana Hills area near Pakistan’s Sargodha, where nuclear-related facilities are located.\nSpeaking at the ‘VIMARSH’ event on Operation Sindoor and its aftermath at the Vivekananda International Foundation, Gen. Chauhan said India had sent several loitering missions around Sargodha before the strikes to search for radars.\nDuring a media briefing during Operation Sindoor, former Director General of Air Operations, Air Marshal A.K. Bharti, had said on May 12 last year that the Indian armed forces did not target the nuclear facility at Kirana Hills in Pakistan.\nResponding to a query during a press conference at the time, Air Marshal Bharti said, “Thank you for telling us that Kirana Hills houses some nuclear installation. We did not know about it. And we have not hit Kirana Hills, whatever is there.”\n“Kirana Hills is about 10 kilometres north of Sargodha,” he said, explaining that the loitering missions had an endurance of around two hours. If they failed to locate a target within that period, they would eventually descend and strike somewhere.\n“It must have come and hit one of those hills,” Gen. Chauhan said, referring to the possibility that one of the loitering munitions struck Kirana Hills. He also referred to images aired by Pakistani media showing smoke emerging from the area.\nGen. Chauhan said India’s operational success during Operation Sindoor was driven by better situational awareness and battlefield transparency, enabled by greater networking among the Army, Navy and Air Force.\n“We were able to make better decisions,” he said, attributing the outcome also to “clear political direction” that gave the armed forces a free hand to plan operations.\nOn Pakistan’s use of satellite imagery, the former CDS said commercial satellite imagery from Chinese companies was available to anyone and suggested that Pakistan had attempted to use such imagery to assess the results of its strikes.\nHe also questioned the accuracy of Pakistan’s battlefield intelligence, particularly regarding maritime operations. Pakistani briefings reportedly claimed that an Indian carrier battle group was at certain locations in the Arabian Sea, but checks with the Western Naval Command showed it was actually 100 to 300 nautical miles away.\nGen. Chauhan attributed this to Pakistan’s dependence on foreign sources, noting that its long-range maritime aircraft did not fly during the period.\nDescribing India’s escalation strategy, he said one of the principles was that the “last strike or the winning note” would always be delivered by India.\nOn U.S. President Donald Trump’s claims of mediating the India-Pakistan ceasefire, Gen. Chauhan said the decision was reached directly between the two Directors General of Military Operations (DGMOs).\n“The ceasefire was decided between two DGMOs, and that’s it. I don’t see the role of anyone else,” he said.\nHe also questioned how information about the impending ceasefire reached the U.S. side before India could announce it, saying he was certain the information had not leaked from the Indian side.\nPublished - August 26, 2026 01:44 am IST",
      "text_source": "full"
    },
    "opportunities-752799fcb5": {
      "headline": "Applications open for Pravaha’s ₹5 crore fully funded postgraduate fellowship for 50 graduates",
      "source": "Newspatrolling.com",
      "url": "https://newspatrolling.com/applications-open-for-pravahas-%E2%82%B95-crore-fully-funded-postgraduate-fellowship-for-50-graduates/",
      "published": "2026-08-25T12:14:46+00:00",
      "image": null,
      "fulltext": "The inaugural one-year residential Fellowship will begin in Vaishali in August 2027. Applications are open nationally, with a preference for candidates from Bihar.\nPatna, 25 August 2026: Pravaha has opened applications for the inaugural cohort of its postgraduate Fellowship, offering 50 graduates and early-career professionals fully funded places backed by a ₹5 crore scholarship commitment.\nThe one-year residential programme will begin at Pravaha’s campus in Loma, Vaishali, in August 2027, with scholarships covering academics, residence and student life for all 50 Fellows.\nDesigned for a workplace increasingly shaped by technology and AI, the Fellowship combines business, data, artificial intelligence, design and leadership with practical projects, mentorship and intensive career preparation. Applications are open nationally, with a preference for candidates from Bihar.\nLearning built around the future of work\nThe Fellowship will deliver 1,200 contact hours across 45 weeks and four terms, combining classroom learning with projects, labs, studios, writing workshops and leadership simulations.\nThe curriculum is designed around capabilities increasingly valued across organisations, including analytical judgement, financial literacy, communication, technology fluency, problem-solving, project execution and leadership.\nFellows will work on financial models, market analyses, dashboards, strategy proposals and AI-enabled workflows, while gaining hands-on exposure to tools including Claude, Figma, Cursor and Excel.\nMore than 150 hours of personalised career preparation will also be built into the programme, preparing Fellows for roles across strategy, growth, data and analytics, product, Founder’s Offices, consulting, public systems and institution building.\nDedicated career support will help Fellows identify the right opportunities, prepare for recruitment and interviews and connect with employers and professional networks, with the objective of launching them into strong, high-quality career pathways.\n“What is distinctive about Pravaha is the way the academic model brings together business, AI, data, design and leadership with serious career preparation. At Jetri, we have worked to translate that ambition into a coherent learning model that is rigorous, applied and aligned with the world of work young people are entering,” said Shreyasi Singh, Founding Managing Partner, Jetri.\nCreating high-quality career pathways from Bihar\nPravaha is being built around a simple proposition: ambitious young people should not necessarily have to leave Bihar to access high-quality learning, professional networks and career opportunities.\nThe initiative brings together Dr Ajay Kumar, who pledged land in Vaishali to establish the institution; Uday Shankar, former CEO of Star India and former President of The Walt Disney Company Asia Pacific; and Dr Pramath Raj Sinha, Co-founder of Pravaha, Founder and Chairperson of the Board of Trustees of Ashoka University and Founding Dean of the Indian School of Business.\n“The brightest students in Bihar will aspire to get into an IIT or an AIIMS, or go to Delhi University, Oxford or Stanford. But they’ll say, ‘Look, while I’m applying to all these places, I think I should also apply to Pravaha,’” said Dr Pramath Raj Sinha.\nThe Fellowship will draw on more than 30 faculty members and practitioners across economics, AI, business, strategy, leadership, communication and public policy, including academics and practitioners associated with institutions such as Harvard, Ashoka University, ISB and Northeastern University.\nApplications and selection\nApplicants must hold a bachelor’s degree in any discipline, be between 21 and 28 years old, have up to five years of work experience and possess working proficiency in English. Selection will include an online assessment followed by interviews for shortlisted candidates.\nApplications opened on August 10, 2026. The first online test is scheduled for September 15, with interviews beginning October 10 and first-round decisions announced on October 30.\nThe founding cohort will begin Fellowship Orientation in Vaishali on August 17, 2027.",
      "text_source": "full"
    },
    "opportunities-ea247bc836": {
      "headline": "Call for Applications: 2027 Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program",
      "source": "Tech Policy Press",
      "url": "https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-policy-press-fellowship-program/",
      "published": "2026-08-24T15:34:13+00:00",
      "image": null,
      "fulltext": "Call for Applications: 2027 Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program\nRamsha Jahangir / Aug 24, 2026\nTech Policy Press is now accepting applications for its 2027 fellowship cohort.\nSince its launch in 2023, the fellowship program has supported 25 fellows from 11 countries across three cohorts, who have written on pressing topics such as the governance of artificial intelligence, tech regulation, digital rights, and the entanglement of state and corporate power. You can learn more about past fellowship cohorts and read their work here.\nOverview\nFor 2027, we intend to support a new cohort of fellows to pursue independent reporting and analysis that explore urgent questions at the intersection of technology and democracy. The year-long, part-time fellowship is designed to support mid-career journalists, researchers, and public policy professionals. We welcome a wide range of contributions, from original reporting to policy analysis.\nApplicants should come prepared with a clear area of focus and a sense of how their work will contribute to informing both the public and decision-makers. We are particularly interested in projects that center communities and regions often excluded from global tech policy conversations.\nEach fellow will receive a $10,000 stipend to support their work. In addition, fellows will join an international cohort for regular feedback and exchange of ideas, receive ongoing editorial guidance, and participate in monthly sessions designed to support and strengthen their work.\nFellowship expectations\nApplicants should have a strong background or expertise in a specific tech policy area or issue, a particular geography, or, more generally, technology and its impact on democracy or society. All fellows will work remotely as independent contractors.\nAll fellows will be expected to:\n- Contribute (on at least a monthly basis) analysis or reporting on issues related to technology and democracy. (While topics can change after the fellowship commences, each fellow should clearly articulate a topic and why they are a good fit to cover it)\n- Attend a mandatory hour-long monthly cohort meeting\n- Attend a monthly check-in meeting with Tech Policy Press’s editorial team to evaluate progress and discuss next steps\n- Collaborate with other fellows and the Tech Policy Press masthead as appropriate\n- Meet all agreed-upon deadlines for outputs\n- Disclose any potential conflicts of interest\nHow to apply\nIf you would like to apply for the fellowship, complete this form by 11:59 p.m. EDT on September 24th, 2026. Required documents include:\n- A curriculum vitae (CV) or resume\n- Cover letter (no more than two pages) that conveys what you hope to accomplish\n- Links to prior publications\n- Two professional references\nInformation Sessions\nWe will host two information sessions for potential applicants via ZOOM. Registration is required. Choose from one of two dates:\n- September 9th, 8 am EDT - Register here\n- September 16th, 7 pm EDT - Register here\nFrequently Asked Questions\n- Who is eligible to apply?\n  - The fellowship is open to journalists, researchers, lawyers, and public policy professionals who are interested in exploring the intersection of technology and democracy. We encourage applicants from the Global Majority and underrepresented communities in media. Candidates should have a demonstrated interest or background in tech policy, media, or related fields, and be committed to producing high-quality, public-interest work during the fellowship.\n- What are the benefits of being a Tech Policy Press fellow?\n  - The opportunity and support to work on a pressing, underreported issue for a year\n  - Develop a significant body of work by the end of the fellowship\n  - Access to an international cohort of past and current fellows\n  - Engage in learning opportunities provided by Tech Policy Press\n  - Develop and maintain a network of sources in your focus area\n- Can you provide a timeline for the application review process and when selected fellows will be notified of their acceptance?\n  - We aim to notify applicants of their status by early December 2026.\n- When does the fellowship start and how long does it last?\n  - The fellowship will commence in January 2027 and last for 12 months.\n- Are you accepting international applicants?\n  - Yes, and we encourage them!\n- Can I share additional information that is not part of the application questions?\n  - We will only assess applications based on the information required in the application form.\n- What counts as published work?\n  - A published link should direct to work that is publicly accessible online and clearly attributed to you. This can include reported pieces, policy or issue analyses, opinion articles, essays, podcasts, newsletters, or data projects. Please note that social media posts and unpublished material, such as class assignments or dissertations, will not be considered.\n- Will there be an interview round for the fellowship?\n  - A select pool of candidates may be invited for an online interview.\n- How does Tech Policy Press plan to promote and showcase the work of fellows to a wider audience?\n  - We publish fellows’ work on the Tech Policy Press website and feature it prominently in our newsletter to reach a broad and engaged audience. Additionally, we highlight fellowship projects through our podcast and at special events throughout the year.\n- How will the stipends be distributed?\n  - Quarterly.\n- Is the 2027 cohort open to those whose main contributions are technical?\n  - Yes. We welcome applications from a variety of disciplines, as long as you can show how your work aligns with Tech Policy Press’s editorial purview.\n- Can we upload pre-print papers or research papers that are not formally published yet?\n  - No.\n- What kinds of projects have past fellows worked on, particularly for candidates from policy, governance, or development backgrounds?\n  - You can learn more about our fellows and their work via the fellowship page on our website.\n- Are there any preferred geographic or thematic focuses for applicants from the Global South?\n  - No.\n- What are some of the criteria to reject an application?\n  - Applications may be rejected if they do not meet eligibility criteria as listed on our 2027 Fellowship announcement.\n- Are co-authors allowed?\n  - Yes, but we recommend submitting at least one solo publication.\n- Are translations allowed?\n  - You can share translated work, but we also require at least one submission to be written in English.\n- Should the monthly submissions be exclusive to Tech Policy Press?\n  - Yes, but you may republish them.\n- How recent should writing samples be?\n  - Within the past three years.\n- Is this fellowship open to candidates outside the USA who are not authorized to work in the USA?\n  - Yes. This fellowship is open to candidates from around the world.\n- Is it disadvantageous to be open to working in multiple formats?\n  - No.\n- Do you have to stick to one theme, or can you write on a variety of topics?\n  - We encourage fellows to respond to news events as they pertain to their area of expertise.\n- How broadly or specifically should applicants define the tech policy area or issue they want to work on?\n  - That is up to your discretion.\n- The place where I work automatically translates our articles into English using AI. Is that a problem?\n  - We require evidence of your own command of English.\n- What should we do to share a published piece behind a paywall?\n  - You must ensure that the pieces you submit are open access.\n- How competitive is the application? \n  - Last year, we received more than 2,000 applications for only 8-10 fellowship spots. Because we receive such a large number of applications, our team has to make difficult decisions. We appreciate you taking the time to apply.\nOther questions\nIf you have other questions about this opportunity, please contact us at fellows@techpolicy.press.",
      "text_source": "full"
    },
    "opportunities-850ab473ad": {
      "headline": "Early Bird Ending for AI and Models of Care in Hospitals Summit",
      "source": "Medical Republic",
      "url": "https://www.medicalrepublic.com.au/early-bird-ending-for-ai-and-models-of-care-in-hospitals-summit/128449",
      "published": "2026-08-25T05:41:36+00:00",
      "image": null,
      "fulltext": "You have until the night of Thursday 27th to snap up best price tickets to Health Services Daily’s upcoming summit about how AI is reshaping our hospitals.\nThe hospital system is not being replaced. It is being restructured from the inside, and faster than most leaders realise.\nAI is no longer a pilot. Virtual care is no longer a workaround. New models are live, generating evidence, and beginning to change what it means to run a hospital.\nHealth Services Daily’s next Summit, How AI and New Models of Care are Rapidly Reshaping Our Hospitals, will bring together leaders across the sector to examine what is actually in play today through live on-stage technology demonstrations and sharp debate, and asks the harder questions about what it means for hospital economics, workforce and patient experience in the five years ahead.\nEarly bird tickets are now on sale for the one day summit followed by networking drinks – book now for best price tickets.  \n  \nWhen: October 29, 2026 \nWhere: Collins Square, Melbourne \nPROGRAM \nEXTENDED EARLY BIRD TICKETS \nJoin us to hear from our expert speakers, including:\n- Professor Euan Wallace, Immediate Past Secretary of the Victorian Department of Health\n- Dr Chris Robinson, National Chief Medical Officer, St Vincent’s Health Australia\n- Grahame Grieve, Principal at Health Intersections Pty Ltd\n- Dr Katharine Bassett, Director, Health Policy, Catholic Health Australia\n- Dr Rob Marshall, Chief Strategy Officer, St Vincent’s Health Australia\n- Natalie Phan, Executive Manager (Health), Quantium & Board Director, Patients Australia\n- Dr Loren Sher, Clinical Director, Victorian Virtual Emergency Department (VVED)\n- Dr Suzie Miller, Emergency Physician and Director of Strategy, Quality, and Technology, Victorian Virtual Emergency Department (VVED)\n- A/Prof Vikram Palit, CEO, Consultmed, Paediatrician, A/Professor Health Systems UNSW\n- Jacob Madden, Assistant Director-General and Chief Data & Analytics Officer, The Australian Centre for Disease Control\n- Dr Jill Freyne, Healthcare Industry Lead, Amazon Web Services (AWS)\n- Dr Chris Sia, Chief Medical Officer, Telecare\n- Nadia Levin, CEO & Managing Director, Research Australia\n- Prof Matt Sabin, Chief Clinical and Consumer Officer, Healthdirect Australia\n- A/Prof Amith Shetty, Clinical Director, System Sustainability and Performance, NSW Ministry of Health\n- Emma Hossack, CEO, MSIA\n- Marc Miller, Senior Director, WTW\n- A/Prof Emily Kirkpatrick, Managing Director, EKology Group\n- Sallyanne Wissmann, CEO, HIMAA\n- Dr Andrew Rochford, Founder and CEO, Trakked Pty Ltd and many more.\nTopics Include:\n- Models of care, structures and approaches that will define the hospital of the future.\n- Growth of hospital-at-home, virtual care, community-based models and the shift towards care closer to the patient.\n- The role of AI from clinical decision-making and automation to digital infrastructure, data and connected care.\n- Workforce, changing roles, capability and how technology will change the way healthcare professionals work.\n- Financial pressures facing hospitals and the opportunities to improve productivity, efficiency and long-term sustainability without compromising care.\n- Patient expectations, and patient-side AI .\n- Interoperability, information sharing and how better access to data can support clinicians, patients and hospital decision-makers.\n- How hospitals can move beyond pilots and implement new technologies, models and ideas at scale.\n- Policy, funding and system-level change.\nReminder that if you are a paid HSD subscriber, you will automatically receive a further 20% off tickets. To redeem this just log into your account and book through the events page.",
      "text_source": "full"
    }
  }
